ok(177,r"$\vec{AB}=(-1,-2,2)$ and $\vec n=(2,3,4)\times(1,\alpha,1)=(3-4\alpha,\,2,\,2\alpha-3)$, so $\vec{AB}\cdot\vec n=8\alpha-13$ and $|\vec n|^2=20\alpha^2-36\alpha+22$. Setting $6(8\alpha-13)^2=25(20\alpha^2-36\alpha+22)$ gives $\alpha^2+3\alpha-4=0$, so $\alpha=1,-4$ and the sum is $-3$.")
fix(178,r"$f'(x)=3x^2+2ax+\frac{b}{x\ln2}$ vanishes at $x=-1,2$, giving $a=-\frac92$, $b=12\ln2$, so $f=x^3-\frac92x^2+12\ln|x|+1$. On $[-2,-\frac12]$: $f(-2)=-25+12(0.7)=-16.6$, $f(-1)=-4.5$, $f(-\frac12)=-0.25-8.4=-8.65$. So $M=-4.5$, $m=-16.6$, and $|M+m|=21.1$.",
 stem=r"Let $x = -1$ and $x = 2$ be the critical points of the function $f(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + b \log_2|x| + 1,\ x \ne 0$. Let $m$ and $M$ respectively be the absolute minimum and the absolute maximum values of $f$ in the interval $\left[-2, -\frac{1}{2}\right]$. Then $|M + m|$ is equal to: (Take $\log_e 2 = 0.7$)")
ok(179,r"$64=63+1\equiv1\pmod 7$, so any power of $64$ leaves remainder $1$.")
ok(180,r"For a point $(-2,y)$: $PS^2=(y-1)^2$ and $PM^2=\frac{(y-2)^2}{5}$. Setting $5(y-1)^2=(y-2)^2$ gives $4y^2-6y+1=0$, so the sum of the ordinates is $\frac64=\frac32$.")
ok(181,r"Note $d\left(\frac{x^2+e^x}{x^2}\right)=\frac{e^x(x-2)}{x^3}dx$. Dividing the equation by $x^3$: $\frac{x^2+e^x}{x^2}dy+y\,d\left(\frac{x^2+e^x}{x^2}\right)=dx$, so $y\cdot\frac{x^2+e^x}{x^2}=x+C$. At $(1,0)$, $C=-1$. Then $y(2)\cdot\frac{4+e^2}{4}=1\Rightarrow y(2)=\frac4{4+e^2}$.")
ok(182,r"With the captain (batsman) and vice-captain (bowler) fixed, choose 8 more from 6 batsmen and 5 bowlers so that the team has (bat, bowl) $=(4,6),(5,5),(6,4)$. That is $\binom63\binom55+\binom64\binom54+\binom65\binom53=20+75+60=155$.")
ok(183,r"Let $u=\tan(\theta+\frac\pi6)=\frac y2$; then $\frac x3=\tan(\theta+\frac\pi3)=\frac{\sqrt3u+1}{\sqrt3-u}$. Clearing denominators gives $xy-2\sqrt3x+3\sqrt3y+6=0$, so $\alpha^2+\beta^2+\gamma^2=12+27+36=75$.")
ok(184,r"$C_1$: centre $(-3,-3)$, radius $3$. The distance to $(1,3)$ is $2\sqrt{13}$. The contact point is $(-3,-3)+\frac{3}{2\sqrt{13}}(4,6)=\left(-3+\frac6{\sqrt{13}},-3+\frac9{\sqrt{13}}\right)$, so $(\beta-\alpha)^2=\frac9{13}$ and $m+n=22$.")
fix(185,r"By $x\to\pi-x$, $\int_0^\pi\frac{x\sin x}{1+3\cos^2x}dx=\frac\pi2\int_0^\pi\frac{\sin x}{1+3\cos^2x}dx$. With $t=\cos x$: $\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{dt}{1+3t^2}=\frac{2}{\sqrt3}\cdot\frac\pi3=\frac{2\pi}{3\sqrt3}$. So $I=\frac\pi2\cdot\frac{2\pi}{3\sqrt3}+3\cdot\frac{2\pi}{3\sqrt3}=\frac{\pi}{3\sqrt3}(\pi+6)$.",
 stem=r"The integral $\int_{0}^{\pi} \frac{(x + 3) \sin x}{1 + 3 \cos^2 x} \, dx$ is equal to:")
ok(187,r"Corrected sum $=4000-10=3990$, so $\mu=39.9$. $\sum x^2=100(5.1^2+1600)=162601$; corrected $=162601-2500+1600=161701$. $\sigma^2=1617.01-1592.01=25$, so $\sigma=5$ and $10(\mu+\sigma)=449$.")
ok(188,r"Take $x_i=a,ar,ar^2,ar^3$. The AP conditions are $2(ar-7)=(a-2)+(ar^2-9)$ and $2(ar^2-9)=(ar-7)+(ar^3-5)$, giving $a=-3$, $r=2$. The terms are $-3,-6,-12,-24$, with product $5184$, so $\frac{5184}{24}=216$.")
ok(189,r"Both roots negative needs $D=(p+2)^2-4(2p+9)\ge0\Rightarrow p\le-4$ or $p\ge8$; sum $p+2<0$; product $2p+9>0$. So $p\in(-\frac92,-4]$, and $\beta-2\alpha=-4+9=5$.")
ok(190,r"$|\text{adj}^3A|=|A|^{8}=81\Rightarrow|A|^2=3$, and $|\text{adj}(\text{adj}A)|=|A|^4$. The equation becomes $|A|^{2(n-1)^2}=|A|^{3n^2-5n-4}\Rightarrow n^2-n-6=0$, so $n=3,-2$. The sum is $|A|^{12}+|A|^{2}=729+3=732$.")
ok(191,r"$4-\frac{x^2}4=\frac{x-4}2\Rightarrow x^2+2x-24=0\Rightarrow x=-6,4$. $\alpha=\int_{-6}^{4}\left(6-\frac x2-\frac{x^2}4\right)dx=\frac{125}{3}$, so $6\alpha=250$.")
ok(192,r"The coefficient determinant is $0\Rightarrow\lambda=5$, and $\Delta_z=0\Rightarrow\mu=9$. The radius is the distance from $(5,9)$ to $4x-3y=0$: $\frac{|20-27|}{5}=\frac75$.")
ok(194,r"$\cos\theta=\sqrt{1-\frac{65}{81}}=\frac49$. $\vec c\cdot\hat a=3+6\cos\theta=\frac{17}{3}$ and $\vec c\cdot\hat b=3\cos\theta+6=\frac{22}{3}$ (the cross-product term drops out). So $9\cdot\frac{17}3-3\cdot\frac{22}3=51-22=29$.")
ok(195,r"$A=(1,1)$, $B=(-2,0)$, $C=(2,0)$. The altitude from $A$ is $x=1$; the altitude from $B$ is perpendicular to $AC$ (slope $-1$), i.e. $y=x+2$. So $P=(1,3)$ and the area of $PBC$ is $\frac12\cdot4\cdot3=6$.")
ok(201,r"$\vec n=(2,3,4)\times(3,4,5)=(-1,2,-1)$, $|\vec n|=\sqrt6$; $(\lambda-1,2,2)\cdot\vec n=3-\lambda$. $\frac{|3-\lambda|}{\sqrt6}=\frac1{\sqrt6}\Rightarrow\lambda=2,4$. The circle through $(0,0),(2,4),(4,2)$ is $x^2+y^2-\frac{10}{3}(x+y)=0$, with radius $\frac53\sqrt2$.")
fix(202,r"The matrix equation gives $4-a-2b=0$, $64-a-14b=0$, so $a=-6$, $b=5$. Since $\alpha^3=1$: $\alpha^{-4}=\alpha^2$, $\alpha^{-a}=\alpha^6=1$, $\alpha^{-b}=\alpha^{-5}=\alpha$. So $4\alpha^2+m+n\alpha=3$; using $\alpha^2=-1-\alpha$, $(m-4)+(n-4)\alpha=3\Rightarrow m=7$, $n=4$, and $m+n=11$.",
 stem=r"Let $\alpha$ be a solution of $x^2 + x + 1 = 0$, and for some a and b in $\mathbb{R}$, $[4 \; a \; b] \begin{bmatrix}1 & 16 & 13 \\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\ -2 & -14 & -8\end{bmatrix} = [0 \; 0 \; 0]$. If $\frac{4}{\alpha^4} + \frac{m}{\alpha^a} + \frac{n}{\alpha^b} = 3$, then m + n is equal to")
fix(203,r"$f'(x)=\frac13-\frac3{x^2}>0\iff|x|>3$. So $f$ increases on $(-\infty,-3)\cup(3,\infty)$ and decreases on $(-3,0)\cup(0,3)$: $\alpha_1=-3,\alpha_2=3,\alpha_3=-3,\alpha_4=0,\alpha_5=3$. $\sum\alpha_i^2=9+9+9+0+9=36$. Trap: $x=0$ is excluded from the domain.",
 stem=r"Let the function $f(x) = \frac{x}{3} + \frac{3}{x} + 3, \; x \ne 0$ be strictly increasing in $(-\infty, \alpha_1) \cup (\alpha_2, \infty)$ and strictly decreasing in $(\alpha_3, \alpha_4) \cup (\alpha_4, \alpha_5)$. Then $\sum_{i=1}^{5} \alpha_i^2$ is equal to:")
ok(204,r"$\beta=\frac1{16}\cdot\frac{\pi^4}{90}$, and $\alpha=\frac{\pi^4}{90}-\beta=\frac{15}{16}\cdot\frac{\pi^4}{90}$. So $\frac\alpha\beta=15$.")
ok(205,r"$P(A\cap B)=0.7-0.5=0.2$, and $P(A\cup\bar B)=0.7+0.6-0.5=0.8$. $B\cap(A\cup\bar B)=A\cap B$, so the probability is $\frac{0.2}{0.8}=\frac14$.")
fix(206,r"Let $t=|x-2|\ge0$: $t^2+t-2=0\Rightarrow t=1$, so $x=1,3$ (squares sum to $10$). For $x^2-2|x-3|-5=0$: if $x\ge3$, $(x-1)^2=0$ gives no valid root; if $x<3$, $x^2+2x-11=0\Rightarrow x=-1\pm2\sqrt3$, both valid (squares sum to $26$). The total is $36$.",
 stem=r"The sum of the squares of the roots of $|x - 2|^2 + |x - 2| - 2 = 0$ and the squares of the roots of $x^2 - 2|x - 3| - 5 = 0$, is:")
ok(207,r"The diagonals meet at the centre $\left(\sqrt3-3,\sqrt3+3\right)$. Its distance from $O$ is $\sqrt{(\sqrt3-3)^2+(\sqrt3+3)^2}=\sqrt{24}$, which is half a diagonal. So the diagonal is $2\sqrt{24}$ and $a^2=\frac{(2\sqrt{24})^2}{2}=48$.")
fix(208,r"Put $t=2x$ in $I_1$: $I_1=\frac12\int_{-1}^{2}t\,f(t(1-t))\,dt$. Replacing $t\to1-t$ (same limits, $f$-argument unchanged) and adding gives $2I_1=\frac12\int_{-1}^{2}f(t(1-t))\,dt=\frac12I_2$. So $\frac{I_2}{I_1}=4$.",
 stem=r"Let $f(x)$ be a positive function and $$I_1 = \int_{-\frac{1}{2}}^{1} 2x f(2x(1 - 2x)) \, dx \quad \text{and} \quad I_2 = \int_{-1}^{2} f(x(1 - x)) \, dx.$$ Then the value of $\frac{I_2}{I_1}$ is equal to:")
ok(209,r"$\vec c\parallel\vec b-\frac{\vec a\cdot\vec b}{|\vec a|^2}\vec a=(2,1,-1)-\frac12(1,2,1)=\frac32(1,0,-1)$. So $\vec c=\pm\frac1{\sqrt2}(\hat i-\hat k)$, which matches $\frac1{\sqrt2}(-\hat i+\hat k)$. Check: it is perpendicular to $\vec a$ and lies in the plane.")
ok(210,r"The centre is $C(1,2)$ and $r=\sqrt{1+4+11}=4$. $3+4p=4\Rightarrow p=\frac14$: $\frac{x^2}{4/3}+\frac{y^2}{16}=1$ (major axis along $y$), $e^2=\frac{11}{12}$. Focal distances are $4\pm2e$, so $f_1f_2=16-\frac{11}{3}=\frac{37}3$ and $6f_1f_2-r=74-4=70$.")
ok(211,r"$x\sin\pi x\ge0$ on $[-1,1]$ and $\le0$ on $[1,\frac32]$. Using $\int\pi^2x\sin\pi x\,dx=\sin\pi x-\pi x\cos\pi x$: $\int_{-1}^{1}=2\pi$, and $\int_1^{3/2}=(-1)-(\pi)=-1-\pi$. So the total is $2\pi+1+\pi=1+3\pi$.")
fix(212,r"After the clockwise rotation, the slope is $\tan\frac\alpha2=2-\sqrt3\Rightarrow\alpha=\frac\pi6$. The line $y=(2-\sqrt3)(x-a)$ has distance $\frac{(2-\sqrt3)|a|}{\sqrt{8-4\sqrt3}}=\frac{|a|\sqrt{2-\sqrt3}}{2}=\frac1{\sqrt2}$ from the origin, so $a^2=2(2+\sqrt3)$. Then $3a^2\tan^2\alpha-2\sqrt3=2(2+\sqrt3)-2\sqrt3=4$.",
 stem=r"A line passing through the point $P(a, 0)$ makes an acute angle $\alpha$ with the positive x-axis. Let this line be rotated about the point P through an angle $\frac{\alpha}{2}$ in the clockwise direction. If in the new position, the slope of the line is $2 - \sqrt{3}$ and its distance from the origin is $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$, then the value of $3a^2 \tan^2 \alpha - 2\sqrt{3}$ is:")
ok(213,r"$\binom{12}{3}-\binom53=220-10=210$. Trap: subtracting only one degenerate triple instead of all $\binom53$.")
ok(214,r"$\mathrm{Re}\frac{(2\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)(\cos\theta+3i\sin\theta)}{\cos^2\theta+9\sin^2\theta}=\frac{2\cos^2\theta-3\sin^2\theta}{1+8\sin^2\theta}$. The condition simplifies to $\frac{21\cos2\theta}{1+8\sin^2\theta}=0$, so $\cos2\theta=0$: $\theta=\frac\pi4,\frac{3\pi}4,\frac{5\pi}4,\frac{7\pi}4$. $\sum\theta^2=\frac{\pi^2}{16}(1+9+25+49)=\frac{21}4\pi^2$.")
fix(215,r"$T_{r+1}=\binom{1016}{r}5^{\frac{1016-r}{2}}7^{\frac r8}$ is integral when $8\mid r$ (then $1016-r$ is automatically even). $r=0,8,\dots,1016$ gives $128$ terms.",
 stem=r"The number of integral terms in the expansion of $\left( 5^{\frac{1}{2}} + 7^{\frac{1}{8}} \right)^{1016}$ is:")
fix(217,r"$g(f(f(x)))=e^{x-2}$, so $y'+\frac{y}{\sqrt x}=e^{x-2-2\sqrt x}$. The integrating factor $e^{2\sqrt x}$ gives $(ye^{2\sqrt x})'=e^{x-2}$, so $ye^{2\sqrt x}=e^{x-2}-e^{-2}$. At $x=1$: $y(1)=e^{-2}(e^{-1}-e^{-2})=\frac{e-1}{e^4}$.",
 stem=r"Let $f(x) = x - 1$ and $g(x) = e^x$ for $x \in \mathbb{R}$. If $$\frac{dy}{dx} = \left( e^{-2\sqrt{x}} g(f(f(x))) - \frac{y}{\sqrt{x}} \right), \quad y(0) = 0,$$ then $y(1)$ is:")
ok(218,r"Since $\cos2<0$, $\sqrt{1+\tan^22}=-\sec2$, so the first argument is $\frac{-\sec2-1}{\tan2}=-\cot1$, and $\cot^{-1}(-\cot1)=\pi-1$. The second argument is $\frac{\sec\frac12+1}{\tan\frac12}=\cot\frac14$, so that term is $\frac14$. The result is $\pi-1-\frac14=\pi-\frac54$. Trap: taking $\sqrt{1+\tan^22}=\sec2$.")
ok(219,r"Apply $R_2\to R_2-2R_1$, $R_3\to R_3-3R_1$, then $R_3\to R_3-3R_2$: the matrix becomes upper triangular with diagonal $2,2,2$, so $|A|=8$. For $3\times3$, $|\text{adj}(\text{adj}\,B)|=|B|^{4}$, and $|3A|=27\cdot8=2^33^3$. So the value is $2^{12}3^{12}$ and $m+n=24$.")
